title: Shared Responsibility Model
page: shared-responsibility-model.html
as-of: 2026-11-25
promise: Most cloud breaches are misunderstandings of where the line sits.

== The 30-second version
layout: text
section: top
The provider secures the platform: data centers, hypervisor, managed-service internals. You secure how you configure and use it: IAM, network rules, data, code, workloads. The line shifts with IaaS, PaaS, SaaS and serverless.
notes:
The cloud provider secures the underlying platform: physical data centers, the hypervisor and managed-service internals. You secure how you configure and use it: IAM, network rules, data, code and workloads.

The dividing line shifts depending on whether you use IaaS, where you secure most of it, PaaS, where you secure less, SaaS, where you secure the least, or serverless, which falls somewhere in between.

The page's through-line is that most cloud breaches are misunderstandings of where the line sits, not provider failures.

== Why the model exists
layout: text
section: why-it-exists
Before public cloud you secured everything from the rack up. The model answers the question AWS's 2006 EC2 launch raised: if Amazon runs my server, is my data their problem?
notes:
Before public cloud, security was conceptually simple: you bought hardware and secured everything from the rack up. When AWS launched EC2 in 2006, it raised an obvious question: if Amazon is running my server, are they responsible for keeping it patched, and is my data their problem?

The shared responsibility model is the answer. It is a contract, partly explicit and partly implicit: the provider handles the things you cannot see and cannot touch, and you handle everything else.

== One model, several versions
layout: list
section: why-it-exists
item: Not a regulation or a single specification
item: Each provider publishes its own version
item: CSA Cloud Controls Matrix is a vendor-neutral attempt
item: NIST SP 800-145 defines IaaS, PaaS and SaaS
item: ISO 27017 codifies it in compliance language
notes:
The model is not a regulatory document or a single specification. Each provider publishes its own version, close in shape but different in detail.

The CSA's Cloud Controls Matrix attempts a vendor-neutral version. NIST SP 800-145 defines the IaaS, PaaS and SaaS service models that the responsibility line traces. ISO 27017 codifies the model in compliance language.

== Where the line sits, by service model
layout: figure
section: by-service-model
figure: srm-figure
The customer's share shrinks as you move up the stack.
notes:
The chart shows five service models and how the customer-versus-provider line shifts from on-premises to SaaS. On-prem is fully customer-owned, IaaS is roughly 70 percent customer, PaaS is split, FaaS is mostly provider, and SaaS is almost entirely provider.

The caption carries the key point: the line shifts as you move up the stack, but data, identity and configuration always stay yours.

== Who owns which layer
layout: table
section: by-service-model
row: Layer | IaaS | PaaS | SaaS
row: Data and IAM | You | You | You
row: Application code | You | You | Provider
row: Runtime and dependencies | You | Shared | Provider
row: Operating system | You | Provider | Provider
row: Hypervisor and hardware | Provider | Provider | Provider
Network controls are yours on IaaS and PaaS, and limited on SaaS.
notes:
This is an excerpt of the page's table, which also has on-premises and FaaS columns. On-premises you own every layer. Data classification and access, and identity, stay with you in every column.

Application code is yours except on SaaS. Runtime and dependencies are yours on IaaS, shared on PaaS and the provider's on SaaS. The operating system is yours only on IaaS. The hypervisor and hardware are the provider's in every cloud model.

Network controls, in configuration terms, are yours on IaaS and PaaS and limited on SaaS.

== IaaS and PaaS
layout: compare
section: by-service-model
col: IaaS | You pick the OS image | You own its patching and hardening | The provider's job ends at "the VM boots and the network works"
col: PaaS | The runtime is patched for you | You own schema, IAM, endpoints and code | A database with a default password is still yours
Cloud Run runs your container image, so its OS packages are yours to patch.
notes:
On IaaS, such as EC2, Azure Virtual Machines and GCE, you picked the OS image, so you own its patching, hardening and runtime monitoring. You also own the security group rules, the VPC layout and the code on top. If the OS in your EC2 instance is unpatched and gets popped, that is not AWS's problem.

On PaaS, such as App Service, RDS and Cloud SQL, the runtime gets patched for you. You still own the database schema, IAM policies, network endpoints, application code and dependencies. A database with a default password is your problem regardless of who is patching MySQL.

Cloud Run is different: it runs the container image you deploy, so the OS packages in that image are yours to patch unless you deploy on Google's buildpack base images with automatic base image updates turned on.

== What you keep on SaaS
layout: list
section: by-service-model
item: User accounts and access, including MFA
item: Data classification and DLP
item: Sharing and collaboration settings
item: Third-party app integrations and OAuth grants
item: Audit log review
notes:
On SaaS you do not see the runtime, pick the version or care about the OS, but you still own a lot. Every M365 admin without MFA is your incident waiting to happen. Salesforce will not stop your sales rep from emailing customer lists. Open-by-default link sharing in OneDrive or Drive is the source of constant data leaks.

You also own the OAuth grants you forgot about. Providers will keep the logs, but whether anyone is watching them is on you.

== Snowflake and UNC5537
layout: text
section: by-provider
Customers could leave MFA off per account. About 165 customer organizations were notified as potentially exposed.
- Pre-2024 default: MFA available, not required
- MFA enrollment later the default for new password users
- Mandatory MFA phased in through 2025 and 2026
notes:
Snowflake is an instructive case. It is SaaS: Snowflake patches the database, runs the infrastructure and manages the underlying clouds. But customers could leave MFA off per account, and the pre-2024 default was MFA available but not required.

That choice put MFA enforcement on the customer's side of the line, and the UNC5537 campaign exploited it. Mandiant and Snowflake found no evidence that Snowflake's own platform was breached. The weak point was customer account configuration.

After the incidents, Snowflake made MFA enrollment the default for password users in new accounts, rolled out from late 2024 into early 2025, and then phased in mandatory MFA for human users who sign in with a password, outside reader and trial accounts, through 2025 and 2026. That moved more of the responsibility to their side.

== Serverless, the awkward in-between
layout: text
section: by-service-model
The provider owns the OS and runtime. The code, dependencies, function IAM permissions and event sources are yours.
- You cannot run an EDR agent on Lambda
- Detection relies on provider telemetry: CloudTrail, function logs, X-Ray
notes:
For Lambda, Cloud Run functions and Azure Functions, the OS, the runtime and, for fully managed serverless, even much of the network is the provider's. But the code is yours, the dependencies are yours, the function's IAM permissions are yours, and the event sources you wire up, such as S3 buckets, API Gateways and queues, are yours.

You cannot run an EDR agent on Lambda. CWPP coverage of serverless workloads relies almost entirely on provider-side telemetry, because there is no syscall stream to monitor. That shifts more of the practical detection burden onto the provider's logging without explicitly shifting the contractual responsibility.

== Reading across AWS, Azure and GCP
layout: figure
section: by-provider
figure: svc-title
Translation is not one to one. Read across vendors; it is not a hard equivalence.
notes:
The figure translates equivalent service names across the three major clouds, by category.

The caption warns that translation is not 1:1. Azure splits IAM into Entra ID for identity and Azure RBAC for authorization, and Cloud Run sits halfway between Lambda and ECS. Use it to read across vendors, not as a hard equivalence.

== Three providers, three framings
layout: compare
section: by-provider
col: AWS | "Security of the cloud" vs "in the cloud" | Per-service responsibility breakdown | IAM is fully customer-side
col: Azure | Tiered diagram across on-prem, IaaS, PaaS, SaaS | Entra ID: Microsoft runs it, you configure it | Built in does not mean turned on
col: Google Cloud | Extends the model with "shared fate" | Assured Workloads, org policy defaults | VPC Service Controls perimeters
notes:
The shape is the same across providers; the framings and edge cases vary.

AWS coined "security of the cloud" versus "security in the cloud" and publishes a per-service responsibility breakdown. IAM is fully customer-side: AWS provides Access Analyzer as help, but every IAM finding is yours to fix. S3 has moved toward secure by default, but the provider's job is the feature existing and yours is using it.

Microsoft publishes a tiered diagram. Entra ID is a Microsoft-managed identity service, but you configure conditional access, MFA, privileged role assignments and OAuth app permissions. Defender for Cloud and Sentinel being built in does not mean they are on.

Google extends the standard model with the shared fate framing, with examples such as Assured Workloads and opinionated organization-policy defaults. VPC Service Controls offer a service perimeter that the page says catches data exfiltration at a layer AWS and Azure have no equivalent for.

== What is always yours
layout: checklist
section: always-yours
check: Data classification
check: Access management
check: Account configuration
check: Third-party integrations
check: Turning on the features the provider offers
check: Compliance evidence
check: Incident response
notes:
Across IaaS, PaaS, SaaS, every provider and every service tier, these stay on the customer's side of the line.

The provider cannot tell you which data is sensitive. Who gets in, with what permissions and what authentication strength, is yours. So is account configuration: public versus private, encryption on or off, logging on or off. Every OAuth grant, connected app and API key you issue is yours.

The provider offers MFA, encryption and logging, and turning them on is yours. The provider gives raw compliance data and you assemble it for your auditor. The provider may detect an incident, but coordinating comms, notifications and disclosure is yours.

== What is always the provider's
layout: checklist
section: always-theirs
check: Physical security of data centers
check: Hypervisor and tenant isolation
check: Hardware-level security
check: Backbone network
check: Managed service internals
check: Logging integrity
notes:
These stay on the provider's side: data centers, badge access, environmental controls and media destruction. Tenant-to-tenant isolation, so cross-tenant escapes are the provider's problem. Server lifecycle and supply-chain integrity at the hardware level.

Also the backbone network: provider-internal links, region-to-region encryption, and BGP and DDoS protection at the edge. The code running managed services such as RDS, Snowflake, Cosmos DB and Cloud Run.

Logging integrity is split: CloudTrail's tamper resistance is AWS's, while durable storage is yours.

== Gotchas that cause incidents
layout: list
section: gotchas
item: "Managed" means different splits for different services
item: Features that are available but not enabled
item: "We offer" is not "we do"
item: Subprocessors one layer further down the chain
item: Region-specific data residency obligations
notes:
Two services with "managed" in the name can have vastly different splits. EKS means AWS manages the control plane while you still manage worker nodes, workload security and pod-level config. Aurora Serverless v2 means AWS manages essentially everything below the schema. Assumptions do not transfer.

Most providers ship features that are available, not enabled: encryption at rest, audit logging, MFA enforcement, access logging, anomaly detection. The provider's responsibility is to provide the feature; yours is to use it. Likewise Conditional Access, IAM Access Analyzer and Security Command Center do nothing until you turn them on, configure them and respond to their findings.

SaaS vendors use other SaaS vendors, so your CRM's email-delivery vendor's security sits one layer further down the chain. For region residency, the provider keeps a region in that region, and you ensure your services, replication and support tickets respect it.

== Storm-0558, a provider-side breach
layout: text
section: gotchas
Most cloud breaches are customer-side. In Storm-0558 (2023), a compromised 2016 Microsoft consumer signing key let the attacker forge tokens and read victims' mail.
notes:
Storm-0558 is one of the few high-profile exceptions to the rule that most cloud breaches are customer-side. A 2016 Microsoft consumer signing key was compromised, allowing the attacker to forge tokens for victim accounts and read their mail through Outlook Web Access.

Microsoft owned the failure. Even so, the customer-side investigation question, did the attacker access our mailboxes, was left to customers because Microsoft initially did not have the logging customers needed. That experience caused Microsoft to change which audit-log fields are available without paid tiers.

== The contractual layer
layout: figure
section: contractual
figure: contractual-layer
Attestations audit the provider, not your configuration.
notes:
The figure shows the contractual layer: the provider agreement, attestations, your customer contracts and BAAs.

The caption says attestations audit the provider. They do not audit your configuration.

== Contracts and gaps
layout: list
section: contractual
item: Provider agreements state the split in binding language
item: SOC 2, ISO 27001, FedRAMP and PCI DSS cover the provider's controls
item: Your customer contracts may flow down more than the provider accepts
item: A HIPAA BAA covers only BAA-eligible services
notes:
Master Service Agreements spell out the split in legally binding language, covering service-level commitments, breach notification timelines, data handling, audit rights and limited liability.

Provider attestations tell you what audited controls the provider has in place. They do not audit your configurations. Your customer may require encryption with customer-managed keys, which the cloud provider does not promise to you, and bridging that gap is on you. Using a non-BAA-eligible service for PHI is your problem.

The practical question when scoping a service is who is responsible for what, who accepts that responsibility contractually, and where the gaps fall.

== Shared fate
layout: text
section: shared-fate
Google introduced "shared fate" in 2021: opinionated defaults and customer-side tooling. Contractual responsibility stays where it is.
- Microsoft and AWS have evolved similarly
- Examples: secure-by-default S3 settings, MFA enforcement defaults
notes:
The original model has a fairness problem: it asks customers to operate enterprise-grade security on infrastructure they did not design. Google introduced the term shared fate in 2021 to extend the model, with opinionated defaults such as organization policies that prevent common misconfigurations, customer-side tooling and partnership on insurance and incident response.

Microsoft and AWS have evolved similarly even without using the term. The trend is toward providers taking more practical responsibility while the contractual responsibility stays where it is.

The page's practitioner take is that this is good: defaults that prevent the default failure modes are an order of magnitude more valuable than any documentation about who is responsible for what.

== Who is responsible if I get DDoSed?
layout: question
section: faq
Think about which layer of the stack the traffic hits.
notes:
This is a question from the page's FAQ. Ask it and let the room answer before moving on.

== The provider takes the network layer
layout: answer
section: faq
Generally the provider absorbs network-layer DDoS and you handle application-layer DDoS. Advanced application-layer protection is a paid add-on.
notes:
Generally, the provider absorbs the network-layer DDoS because it hits their pipes, and you handle the application-layer DDoS because the requests are legitimate-looking traffic to your app.

AWS Shield Standard, Azure DDoS infrastructure protection and Cloud Armor's standard tier handle the network layer. Advanced application-layer protection is a paid add-on you would typically buy if you are a high-value target.

== Where does Kubernetes fit?
layout: question
section: faq
Consider the control plane and the worker nodes separately.
notes:
This is the first question in the page's FAQ. Ask it before showing the answer.

== Roughly PaaS for the control plane, IaaS for workers
layout: answer
section: faq
Managed Kubernetes (EKS, AKS, GKE) is roughly PaaS for the control plane and IaaS for the workers. Pod security, Network Policies, RBAC and admission control are all yours.
notes:
The provider patches the API server. You patch the worker node images and operate the workloads. Pod-level security, Network Policies, RBAC and admission control are all yours.
